{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-ae95e971d4e44dad", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787748131.709387, "trigger_id": "trg-074292d1e7385dad9b86d086", "decision": "route", "agent": "research", "endpoint": "http://localhost:8086/research/campaign/a2a", "route_id": "R-blog-summary", "property_name": "blog_summary", "property_value": "SR 11-7 was replaced — and it left generative AIout of scope.\n\nOn April 17, 2026, the OCC, Federal Reserve, and FDIC jointly replaced the 2011 SR 11-7 model-risk-management framework with new guidance (OCC Bulletin 2026-13, Federal Reserve SR 26-2) that is principles-based rather than prescriptive. The new guidance explicitly states that generative and agentic AI models are 'not within the scope' of the guidance, while signaling a forthcoming Request for Information (RFI) on AI-specific model-risk management. This does not exempt banks from oversight of these systems; instead, it shifts the burden of proof from demonstrating compliance with a checklist to demonstrating that the system is actively controlled and governed. The document argues that institutions should build governance as an engineered property of the system—covering grounding, human oversight, audit trails, and least-privilege access—so that evidence of control is produced automatically rather than assembled for an exam. It recommends starting with human-in-the-loop augmentation and expanding AI autonomy only as trust is earned, positioning institutions to be ready when AI-specific regulatory expectations eventually arrive.\n\nKey points:\n- SR 11-7, the 2011 model-risk-management framework, was replaced on April 17, 2026 by OCC Bulletin 2026-13 and Federal Reserve SR 26-2.\n- The new guidance is principles-based rather than prescriptive and explicitly excludes generative and agentic AI models from its scope.\n- Exclusion from scope does not mean exemption from regulatory oversight; banks must still manage AI risk under broader safety-and-soundness and governance expectations.\n- The agencies signaled a forthcoming Request for Information (RFI) on AI-specific model-risk management.\n- The burden of proof has shifted from 'did you follow the framework' to 'can you demonstrate the system is controlled.'\n- Examiners are expected to ask open-ended, behavioral questions about grounding, review, permissions, and incident detection rather than checklist-based questions.\n- A recommended control layer includes grounding/citations, human-in-the-loop review, audit trails, least privilege/role-based access, and monitoring/change control.\n- The lowest-risk deployment path is to start with human-supervised augmentation and expand AI autonomy only as trust is earned.\n- Institutions that build governance controls now will be better positioned when AI-specific regulatory requirements eventually arrive.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, SR 11-7, OCC Bulletin 2026-13, Federal Reserve SR 26-2", "event_id": "2184867559", "object_id": "330395783871", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-ae95e971d4e44dad", "event": "protocol_conversion", "ts": 1787748133.7746139, "trigger_id": "trg-074292d1e7385dad9b86d086", "conversion": "https_ingress -> a2a_message_send", "payload_size_bytes": 3160, "payload_contents": "trigger_id + correlation ids only", "agent": "research", "route_id": "R-blog-summary", "outcome": "dispatched"}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-ae95e971d4e44dad", "event": "run_summary", "ts": 1787748133.7751775, "trigger_id": null, "events_received": 1, "routed": 1, "discarded": 0, "routing_errors": 0, "discards_by_reason": {}, "dispatched_ok": 1, "dispatched_failed": 0, "duration_ms": 2067.9}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-412da9bc71644a55", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787748177.7583869, "trigger_id": "trg-56daefbbc1fe5092bdd1ff56", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "Capital One completed its acquisition of Brex on April 7, 2026 — the same week the OCC and Federal Reserve replaced SR 11-7 with new model-risk guidance. That timing is the story. Brex is now positioned inside Capital One as an AI-native platform that leverages AI agents to help customers automate complex workflows, reduce manual review, and control spend — precisely the agentic AI category that the revised guidance explicitly excludes from its formal scope on the basis that these technologies are \"novel and rapidly evolving.\" The exclusion is not a safe harbor: vendor LLMs and AI agents are evaluated as third-party relationships, with the expectation that the bank — not the vendor — owns the outcome. That puts Capital One's examiners squarely in a position to ask behavioral governance questions about Brex's agentic workflows, and Brex's own product team needs defensible answers about grounding, audit trails, human-in-the-loop review, and permission controls before those examiner conversations happen, because institutions that build defensible governance now will be positioned to deploy AI faster and more safely than peers that treat model risk as a compliance exercise. Opening question for the rep: \"Now that Brex's agentic expense and accounting automation sits inside a federally regulated bank, how is your team thinking about building audit-ready governance into those AI workflows before examiners start asking?\"", "event_id": "12431475", "object_id": "533970643697", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
