{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-412da9bc71644a55", "event": "run_summary", "ts": 1787748182.54373, "trigger_id": null, "events_received": 1, "routed": 1, "discarded": 0, "routing_errors": 0, "discards_by_reason": {}, "dispatched_ok": 1, "dispatched_failed": 0, "duration_ms": 4786.51}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-6574d32105474746", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787748201.666963, "trigger_id": "trg-293c46b6cd565f2b825a1e81", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "Ramp is a finance automation platform serving over 50,000 customers, having built a comprehensive suite of AI agents to automate manual financial workflows including expense policy enforcement, accounting classification, and invoice processing. In June 2026, Ramp launched its Applied AI Solutions offering, designed specifically to help larger enterprises deploy AI agents for complex financial workflows. Their Policy Agent, which uses LLMs to interpret and enforce expense policies written in natural language, demonstrates significant production deployment — including human-in-the-loop labeling sessions and careful context engineering. This puts Ramp squarely in the crosshairs of the regulatory moment the post describes: SR 26-2 carved agentic AI out of model risk scope in April 2026, but that didn't make the risk disappear — it moved the governance burden entirely onto the deploying organization. Because Ramp both operates agentic AI internally and sells agentic finance automation to tens of thousands of businesses — many of them regulated — it sits on both sides of the governance gap. Vendor LLMs are evaluated as third-party relationships, with the expectation that the bank — not the vendor — owns the outcome, which means Ramp's enterprise bank and fintech customers will increasingly scrutinize what audit trails and control evidence Ramp's agents actually produce. The natural opening question for a rep: *When your enterprise customers ask you to demonstrate that Ramp's AI agents are controlled and auditable end-to-end, what does that evidence currently look like — and who inside Ramp owns that answer?*", "event_id": "819902916", "object_id": "534004984568", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-6574d32105474746", "event": "protocol_conversion", "ts": 1787748205.3008616, "trigger_id": "trg-293c46b6cd565f2b825a1e81", "conversion": "https_ingress -> a2a_message_send", "payload_size_bytes": 2230, "payload_contents": "trigger_id + correlation ids only", "agent": "email", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "outcome": "dispatched"}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-6574d32105474746", "event": "run_summary", "ts": 1787748205.3013794, "trigger_id": null, "events_received": 1, "routed": 1, "discarded": 0, "routing_errors": 0, "discards_by_reason": {}, "dispatched_ok": 1, "dispatched_failed": 0, "duration_ms": 3635.59}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-1926e87fc8d649a3", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787748226.6808467, "trigger_id": "trg-431fb943cbb15bc987fe997b", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "MoneyLion, now operating as a unit of Gen Digital following its April 2025 acquisition, has been explicitly built around AI-powered financial wellness, with an AI recommendation system at the core of its consumer marketplace. The company scales AI applications across fraud prevention, budgeting, and personalization, with a centralized approach to experimentation, serving, and governance. That AI-first posture sits squarely in the regulatory gap created by the new model risk framework: the 2026 guidance explicitly excludes generative and agentic AI from its formal scope, describing those systems as novel and rapidly evolving, while a forthcoming RFI on banks' use of AI remains unpublished. SR 26-2 carved agentic AI out of model risk scope — but that didn't make the risk disappear; it moved the governance burden entirely onto the institution. MoneyLion also carries documented regulatory sensitivity: the CFPB reached a $1.75 million settlement with MoneyLion over charges that it overcharged military personnel on loans. With a new AI-powered financial marketplace checkout feature already launched and core systems fully merged with Gen, the pressure to demonstrate AI control to examiners — not just assert it — is immediate and real. A strong opening question: *When MoneyLion's AI recommendation and credit systems come up in an exam or internal audit, what evidence of control does the team currently produce, and how much of that is assembled manually versus built into the system itself?*", "event_id": "2820284400", "object_id": "533995992811", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
