{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-70c83471887545b2", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787748250.5594332, "trigger_id": "trg-f49cecdb351c56f7aecf82c1", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "Stash is a consumer fintech and registered investment adviser (RIA) that serves more than one million subscribers managing over $5 billion in assets, delivering personalized financial guidance at scale through its AI Money Coach product. The company is in the middle of significant corporate change: Grab Holdings announced in February 2026 it would acquire Stash, framing it as an AI-powered investing app designed with existing U.S. regulatory requirements at its core. That framing matters because Stash's AI is central to its commercial story — AI Money Coach has driven strong engagement since its late-2024 launch, with approximately one in two users taking a positive financial action on the same day. The regulatory shift in April 2026 (SR 26-2 / OCC 2026-13) lands directly on Stash's operations: SR 26-2 carved agentic AI out of model risk scope, but that didn't make the risk disappear — it moved the governance burden entirely onto the institution. As an SEC/FINRA-regulated RIA scaling an agentic AI advice tool to retail customers, Stash faces examiner scrutiny that will ask not whether it followed a checklist but whether it can demonstrate the system is actively controlled. The Head of Operations is the natural owner of that evidence layer — audit trails, human-review checkpoints, access controls — especially ahead of a pending Grab acquisition close that will invite heightened regulatory review. Opening question: *How are you currently documenting that AI Money Coach's recommendations stay within appropriate guardrails, and what would you show an examiner today if they asked?*", "event_id": "469944745", "object_id": "534029838017", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
