{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-c21b0b82b8314d05", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787748274.7614098, "trigger_id": "trg-b9d9fb8e8b4a51c79f3fd936", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "M1 Finance is a Chicago-based fintech platform with assets under management rising to over $12–12.5B by late 2025/early 2026 and a user base exceeding one million, built around automated features like Auto-Invest and Dynamic Rebalancing that automatically allocate and rebalance portfolio positions. The platform also has an AI assistant in beta, meaning AI-driven decisioning is already embedded — or actively being embedded — in customer-facing workflows. This puts M1 squarely in the territory the post addresses: SR 26-2 excludes generative AI and agentic AI from its formal scope because these technologies are novel and rapidly evolving, but as one analysis puts it, a gap in supervisory guidance is not a gap in risk — if a generative AI use case fails, the loss, the customer harm, and the reputational cost are unchanged by the fact that the framework no longer names it. M1 operates as a broker-dealer and registered investment adviser, making it subject to safety-and-soundness oversight regardless of the SR 26-2 carve-out. The post's argument — that institutions should engineer governance controls (audit trails, human-in-the-loop review, least-privilege access) as a native property of AI systems rather than assembling evidence for an exam — is directly relevant to M1 as it scales its AI assistant and automated portfolio logic ahead of the agencies' forthcoming RFI on AI-specific model risk. The one question a rep could open with: **As M1 expands its AI assistant and automated decisioning features, how is the team currently thinking about demonstrating control over those systems to examiners who will ask behavioral questions rather than check a compliance box?**", "event_id": "4242619449", "object_id": "533967041217", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
