{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-81dd1d08b9994e6e", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787750379.9718733, "trigger_id": "trg-dba9f65b66b05cc1b9f4da9a", "decision": "route", "agent": "research", "endpoint": "http://localhost:8086/research/campaign/a2a", "route_id": "R-blog-summary", "property_name": "blog_summary", "property_value": "Congress moved to repeal an AI care-denial pilot. Here's what it means for healthcare AI.\n\nOn May 20, 2026, Sen. Ron Wyden and 20 Senate Democrats introduced a Congressional Review Act resolution to repeal CMS's WISeR Model, an AI-assisted prior-authorization pilot for Traditional Medicare implemented January 1, 2026 across six states, following a GAO determination that the pilot is subject to the CRA. A House companion resolution and House Appropriations Committee action followed, though the repeal effort remains unresolved. The article argues the controversy centers not on AI accuracy but on governance: whether a human reviews the output, whether reasoning is visible, and whether a record exists when AI touches a care decision. It draws a distinction between AI that drives a denial decision versus AI that assembles evidence for a human who retains accountability, and presents the latter as the defensible design. The piece concludes that healthcare organizations should use AI to speed up human decision-making rather than replace it, with grounding, human-in-the-loop review, and audit trails as core safeguards.\n\nKey points:\n- Sen. Ron Wyden and 20 Senate Democrats introduced a CRA resolution on May 20, 2026 to repeal CMS's WISeR Model.\n- WISeR is an AI-assisted prior-authorization pilot for Traditional Medicare, implemented January 1, 2026 across six states.\n- A GAO determination found the pilot subject to the Congressional Review Act, enabling the repeal vote.\n- A House companion resolution and House Appropriations Committee action also moved against the pilot; the outcome is unresolved.\n- The controversy centers on governance (human review, transparency, audit trail), not model accuracy.\n- A key distinction is drawn between AI that drives/denies decisions versus AI that assembles evidence for human-owned decisions.\n- The reputational and regulatory risk now includes being seen to automate a denial, regardless of technical correctness.\n- Recommended safeguards: grounding/citations to source material, human-in-the-loop design, end-to-end audit trails, least-privilege access, and transparency over automation.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, WISeR Model (Wasteful and Inappropriate Service Reduction Model), CMS (Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services)", "event_id": "864570265", "object_id": "330390352632", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
