{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-68c0cb33d1694e93", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787750515.2813659, "trigger_id": "trg-00c6427e2a7d50c69d48aa65", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "Cedar is an AI-built healthcare financial experience platform that unifies billing, payments, coverage, and support into a single intelligent solution — with Cedar Intelligence at its core, combining deep healthcare financial data and advanced learning models to continuously adapt to patients and providers. Its recent product expansions include Kora, an AI voice agent to automate patient interactions. Cedar has also expanded Cedar Intelligence to personalize patient billing experiences at scale, with an explicit focus on improving collections and reducing cost-to-collect. This puts Cedar squarely in the crosshairs of the AI governance debate our post addresses: the WISeR controversy has amplified congressional and provider concern about AI systems that drive denial or financial decisions autonomously — particularly where technology partners are rewarded based in part on the volume of care denied. Cedar's AI touches patient financial decisions at scale — coverage screening, billing nudges, payment routing — and the WISeR backlash makes the governance architecture of those workflows newly scrutinized territory. With over 10 million Americans facing potential Medicaid coverage loss, Cedar's patient base is also shifting toward higher-risk, underinsured populations, raising the stakes of any automated coverage or billing decision that could be perceived as a denial. The opening question for a rep: **\"As Cedar Intelligence takes on more of the coverage and financial decision workflow, how are you thinking about where the human review checkpoints sit — and whether your audit trail would hold up if a regulator asked how a specific patient outcome was reached?\"**", "event_id": "2768971244", "object_id": "533965249249", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
