{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-ccccd79827b74c66", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787751464.908744, "trigger_id": "trg-4dfec4b487aa5629bb24c6a1", "decision": "route", "agent": "research", "endpoint": "http://localhost:8086/research/campaign/a2a", "route_id": "R-blog-summary", "property_name": "blog_summary", "property_value": "Congress Moved to Repeal an AI Care-Denial Pilot: What It Means for Healthcare AI | AI Definitive\n\nOn May 20, 2026, Sen. Ron Wyden and 20 Senate Democrats introduced a Congressional Review Act resolution to repeal CMS's WISeR Model, an AI-assisted prior-authorization pilot for Traditional Medicare that launched January 1, 2026 in six states, following a GAO determination that the pilot is subject to CRA review. A House companion resolution and House Appropriations Committee action followed, though the repeal effort remains unresolved. The core objection is not the AI's accuracy but the lack of governance: no clear human review, transparency into reasoning, or audit trail when AI influences a care denial or delay decision. The article argues this signals a broader trust gap in enterprise AI, distinguishing between AI that renders/drives decisions (high risk) versus AI that assembles evidence for a human who retains ownership of the decision (lower risk, defensible design). It concludes that healthcare organizations should design AI deployments with grounding/citations, human-in-the-loop control, end-to-end audit trails, least-privilege access, and transparency to avoid similar legislative and reputational liability.\n\nKey points:\n- Sen. Ron Wyden and 20 Senate Democrats introduced a CRA resolution on May 20, 2026 to repeal CMS's WISeR Model.\n- WISeR is a Traditional Medicare AI-assisted prior-authorization pilot implemented January 1, 2026 across six states.\n- A GAO determination found the pilot subject to the Congressional Review Act, enabling the repeal vote.\n- A House companion resolution and House Appropriations Committee action have also moved to block the pilot.\n- The repeal effort is unresolved as of the article's writing; reporting corroborated by STAT News and Healthcare Dive.\n- The controversy centers on governance (human review, transparency, audit trail), not model accuracy.\n- A key distinction is drawn between AI that drives/renders decisions versus AI that assembles evidence for human decision-makers.\n- The article positions this as a broader signal that ungoverned AI touching care decisions is now a legal-political liability.\n- The author's firm advocates deploying AI (e.g., Claude) with grounding/citations, human-in-the-loop design, audit trails, least-privilege access, and transparency as the opposite governance model.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, CMS WISeR Model, Medicare", "event_id": "3912357215", "object_id": "330422947562", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
