{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-ee7bbf120fe54e8b", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787751607.1638076, "trigger_id": "trg-695a7f83f5a55b4da28fe4b6", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "Cedar is an AI-native patient financial platform that sits squarely at the intersection of healthcare billing, AI automation, and the governance scrutiny the post is about. In April 2026, Cedar announced a major expansion of Cedar Intelligence, its AI-powered decision engine, with new capabilities designed to help providers personalize billing journeys at scale, improve collections, and reduce cost-to-collect. Just two weeks ago, Cedar launched the Kora Platform, an evolution from a single AI billing agent to a suite of purpose-built AI agents that work together to recover patient revenue and reduce cost-to-collect. This aggressive AI expansion is happening precisely when the regulatory environment is tightening hard: states are leading regulation of AI in healthcare in 2026, enacting laws that govern insurers' use of AI in prior authorization and claims decisions, increase transparency requirements, and require human oversight for adverse determinations. The WISeR backlash — centered not on AI accuracy but on ungoverned decision-making — maps directly onto Cedar's domain: some states now prohibit healthcare providers from utilizing AI to submit claims without a review by a provider or billing professional, with such laws already taking effect in 2026. Cedar's own messaging emphasizes human-centered design alongside automation, but as Kora Platform scales toward autonomous patient financial resolution, provider clients and regulators will increasingly want proof of audit trails and human-in-the-loop controls — not just performance metrics. If AI plays a part in a payment error, regulators and plaintiffs usually start with a blunt question: who was responsible for reviewing that output — and if there's no clear human accountability or escalation path, assigning liability gets messy fast. **Opening question for the rep:** As Cedar scales Kora Platform across provider billing workflows, what does Cedar's governance architecture look like for ensuring human accountability and audit trails when AI makes or influences a financial resolution decision affecting a patient?", "event_id": "2348585984", "object_id": "533965249249", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
