{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-2f1e65759ccb4b09", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787755636.6996992, "trigger_id": "trg-c8d486200c67560593117a5d", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "Mahesh Venkatagiri holds a VP role at what was Accolade, now operating inside Transcarent following the April 2025 $621M merger. The combined entity serves over 20 million members and 1,700 clients, with Transcarent's AI-driven navigation layered on top of Accolade's health advocacy infrastructure. The platform pulls personalized care guidance from employer data, health plans, and electronic health records — meaning it is built on a dense web of APIs exchanging sensitive member data across multiple integrated systems. Deep integration with point solution partners across mental health, diabetes, fertility, and musculoskeletal care compounds that API surface area significantly. In a post-merger environment where two engineering orgs are actively converging, production API observability becomes critical: teams need to know exactly which requests are failing, how latency is distributed across endpoints, and how application-level events map back to individual member interactions. Apitally's default of not capturing sensitive data makes it noteworthy for environments with strict privacy requirements — a real differentiator for a health platform handling PHI at scale. The one question a rep could open with: \"As Transcarent and Accolade's engineering teams continue to integrate, how are you currently getting visibility into API errors and performance issues across the combined platform?\"", "event_id": "3483185817", "object_id": "533994194677", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
