{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-ff046e9bfe674082", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787755657.8909183, "trigger_id": "trg-3e6acc36ec535cdfbd31296c", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "Innovaccer is one of the most active builders of agentic AI infrastructure in healthcare right now. The company has committed $250 million to expand its AI agent platform, which automates workflows across prior authorization, revenue cycle management, and population health. This all runs on their \"Gravity\" platform — a foundational layer continuously trained on real-world healthcare data, pulling from customers' EHRs, claims systems, CRM platforms, and finance systems. As of July 2026, Innovaccer was named a Leader in the inaugural Gartner Magic Quadrant for Healthcare Provider Industry Cloud Platforms, and at HIMSS 2026 launched an autonomous medical coding solution (Flow Capture) that handles approximately 80% of encounters without human intervention — all of which points to a team shipping production APIs at serious scale and velocity. This is exactly where robust API logging and observability become critical: with multiple AI agents operating autonomously across clinical and revenue cycle workflows, any silent failure or degraded endpoint could have material downstream consequences. Our FastAPI logging guide covers request logs, application logs, and how to correlate both so every log record is linked back to the specific API request — a concern that is acute when EHR, claims, and finance data flows are all in motion simultaneously. A useful opening question for the rep: *When your AI agents are running autonomously across revenue cycle and clinical workflows at customer health systems, how does your engineering team currently get visibility into API-level failures before they surface as a patient or billing 