{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-1e1e8a2d03b44312", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787756388.304467, "trigger_id": "trg-d83cfdfc4c145531b14283c7", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "**lead_context — Austin Terry, Managing Director, Cedar**\n\nOur post covers structured FastAPI request logging and log correlation in production — exactly the kind of observability infrastructure that matters to a platform handling billions of patient financial interactions at scale. Cedar's platform unifies billing, payments, coverage, and support into a single solution that continuously learns and adapts, which means its engineering teams are maintaining multiple interconnected services where API-level visibility directly affects both uptime and compliance. Cedar's engineers work primarily in Python and TypeScript, making FastAPI a plausible part of their stack, and the company leads technical integrations of its platform with client healthcare systems, managing complex data structures and operational workflows — integrations where a silent failure or misconfigured request can mean lost patient payments or dropped coverage enrollments. Cedar recently expanded its Cedar Intelligence AI platform to personalize the patient financial experience, and launched Kora, an AI voice agent to automate patient billing — both products that introduce new API surfaces where request-level debugging and correlated logs become critical. Apitally by default doesn't capture sensitive data, making it suitable for environments with strict privacy requirements — a strong fit for HIPAA-governed healthcare infrastructure. A good opening question for a rep: *When a billing or coverage API call fails silently in production today, what does your team's process look like for tracing it back to the root cause?*", "event_id": "3448182473", "object_id": "533965249249", "attempt_number": 2, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
