{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-7b67c91ab8254f8a", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787757959.0261483, "trigger_id": "trg-fc6d509777715fdb8fa8bbf9", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "**Sources used:**\n- The published post is a guide to FastAPI logging in production, covering both request logs (recording individual API requests and responses) and application logs (containing detailed messages from application code).\n- The post emphasizes correlating both log types and covers setup from basic Python logging to structured output with tools like structlog or Loguru.\n- Health Catalyst recently sold its Vitalware mid-revenue-cycle business for $147 million and plans to use the proceeds to repay debt, sharpening focus on AI, analytics, and its core technology platform.\n- Health Catalyst has released 10 AI-integrated Data Toolkits on Databricks Marketplace.\n- The Ignite platform includes pre-built standard APIs to provide fast access to data for unified healthcare data.\n- Ignite is built on Azure Databricks and is designed as an all-in-one platform for healthcare organizations.\n- Ignite has introduced built-in observability and intelligent alerting, enabling analytics teams to detect issues proactively and respond faster.\n- Clinical Cost Intelligence, one of Health Catalyst's flagship solutions, uses ML-driven variation analysis and LLM-enabled opportunity screening.\n\n---\n\n### `lead_context` note\n\nHealth Catalyst is in the midst of a deliberate sharpening of its core business: it sold its Vitalware revenue-cycle unit for $147 million to concentrate squarely on AI, analytics, and its core technology platform. That platform — Ignite, built on Azure Databricks — exposes pre-built standard APIs to give downstream systems fast access to unified healthcare data, and the company has shipped 10 AI-integrated Data Toolkits on Databricks Marketplace. With AI agents and LLM-enabled opportunity screening now embedded in flagship products like Clinical Cost Intelligence, Health Catalyst's engineering teams are almost certainly running Python-heavy, API-first microservices at scale — exactly the environment where production FastAPI logging discipline matters most. Ignite already touts built-in observability and intelligent alerting so teams can detect pipeline issues proactively, but that coverage at the data-platform layer doesn't automatically extend to the FastAPI services sitting in front of it. Our post on structured request logging, correlation IDs, and tools like structlog speaks directly to the gap between platform-level observability and API-level debuggability. **Opening question for the rep:** When Health Catalyst's engineering teams troubleshoot issues in the API layer that feeds Ignite or surfaces AI insights to clients, what does their current request-logging and log-correlation setup look like today?", "event_id": "2576786600", "object_id": "533990588137", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
