{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-08a480c2c8544733", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787758030.5457132, "trigger_id": "trg-7b6d4f318dd75b568a96124e", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "**lead_context — Govardhan Terli, Managing Director, Health Catalyst (HCAT)**\n\nHealth Catalyst is a publicly traded healthcare intelligence company (Nasdaq: HCAT) designed to accelerate measurable improvement for health systems. The company is currently in the middle of a significant platform transition: its flagship product, Health Catalyst Ignite™, is a scalable healthcare data and analytics platform that unifies clinical, financial, and claims data into a standardized model, enabling self-service analytics, embedded AI, and faster enterprise decision making. Critically, Ignite ensures faster insights by reducing repetitive data management tasks, streamlining data ingestion from over 300 source templates, and offering pre-built standard APIs. This API layer is directly relevant to our post: for APIs, logging can be broken down into request logs, which record individual API requests and responses, and application logs, which contain detailed messages from the application's code — both of which are essential for a platform like Ignite operating in high-stakes clinical and financial environments. Analysts are watching Health Catalyst's ongoing transition from its legacy DOS platform to Ignite closely, and are factoring in the earnings impact of this migration as part of a broader strategic review. As Health Catalyst pushes more customers onto Ignite's API-driven architecture, observability and structured request logging become operational necessities, not afterthoughts. A strong opening question for a rep: *As Health Catalyst scales the Ignite platform and onboards more health systems onto its API layer, how does your engineering team currently handle request-level visibility and debugging when issues arise in production?*", "event_id": "1669135956", "object_id": "533990588137", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
