{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-8ff1bd68511d4e45", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787758057.1044466, "trigger_id": "trg-6c91043ab3e65406a26d1117", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "**Sources used:**\n- The Apitally post is a complete guide to FastAPI logging in production, covering request logs (individual API requests/responses) and application logs (internal code messages), and how to correlate them.\n- Transcarent completed its merger with Accolade in April 2025; the combined organization now serves over 20 million members and more than 1,700 employer and health plan clients.\n- The new organization blends Transcarent's AI-powered WayFinding system with Accolade's established strength in health advocacy, medical opinion, and virtual care.\n- Healthcare API observability carries specific obligations: HIPAA-compliant trace ingestion (PHI handling at the wire), retention that survives a HIPAA Security Rule audit, and per-clinician access controls that map to clinical workflow.\n- Observability shifts compliance from a periodic review into a live control — but only when it creates a durable trail, not just a noisy alert feed.\n\n---\n\n**`lead_context` note:**\n\nMahesh is a VP at what was Accolade — now fully absorbed into Transcarent following the April 2025 $621M merger — a combined platform serving over 20 million members and 1,700 employer and health plan clients, built around AI-powered care navigation, health advocacy, and virtual primary care. That merger means the engineering and platform teams are actively integrating two distinct backend systems, almost certainly running Python-based microservices and APIs that handle protected health information at scale. Our FastAPI logging guide lands directly in that context: structured, correlated request-and-application logs are the operational foundation for debugging a newly unified platform, and in healthcare, they also carry a compliance obligation — HIPAA requires a durable, auditable record of every API access event, not just alerting. Generic logging setups typically miss PHI-safe data masking and the request-level correlation needed to satisfy a compliance review. The one question worth opening with: *When you're troubleshooting an incident that crosses the legacy Accolade and Transcarent API boundaries, how confident is the team that logs give you full request-level visibility without exposing member PHI in the process?*", "event_id": "2148447962", "object_id": "533994194677", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
