{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-33e51a88b2e24464", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787758363.136282, "trigger_id": "trg-6b49a6a8818b5691936cf47f", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "**lead_context — Govardhan Terli, Managing Director, Health Catalyst**\n\nHealth Catalyst is actively migrating its core data and analytics capabilities from its legacy Data Operating System (DOS) to the cloud-based Ignite™ platform. Ignite streams data from over 300 source templates and exposes pre-built standard APIs, meaning the engineering teams supporting this transition are building and operating a growing surface area of FastAPI-style Python services that connect clinical, financial, and operational data to downstream consumers. Analysts cite the DOS-to-Ignite transition as a key driver of current operational complexity and risk, which implies the platform engineering org is under real pressure to keep those APIs reliable and debuggable in production. Our post — a complete guide to FastAPI logging, covering structured output, request/response correlation, and tools like Apitally — lands squarely on that pain: logging is critical for understanding and debugging FastAPI apps in production, and when things go wrong, logs are usually the first place engineers look. Data masking and sensitive-data controls add a HIPAA-relevant angle that is directly material for a healthcare data platform. A strong opening question for the rep: *As Health Catalyst scales Ignite's API layer across more health system clients, how is the engineering team currently handling structured logging and request traceability across those production services?*", "event_id": "2708808373", "object_id": "533990588137", "attempt_number": 0, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-0af62c447aff48c8", "event": "event_discarded", "ts": 1787758375.4333973, "trigger_id": null, "decision": "discard", "reason": "duplicate_event", "event_id": "2708808373", "object_id": "533990588137", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "**lead_context — Govardhan Terli, Managing Director, Health Catalyst**\n\nHealth Catalyst is actively migrating its core data and analytics capabilities from its legacy Data Operating System (DOS) to the cloud-based Ignite™ platform. Ignite streams data from over 300 source templates and exposes pre-built standard APIs, meaning the engineering teams supporting this transition are building and operating a growing surface area of FastAPI-style Python services that connect clinical, financial, and operational data to downstream consumers. Analysts cite the DOS-to-Ignite transition as a key driver of current operational complexity and risk, which implies the platform engineering org is under real pressure to keep those APIs reliable and debuggable in production. Our post — a complete guide to FastAPI logging, covering structured output, request/response correlation, and tools like Apitally — lands squarely on that pain: logging is critical for understanding and debugging FastAPI apps in production, and when things go wrong, logs are usually the first place engineers look. Data masking and sensitive-data controls add a HIPAA-relevant angle that is directly material for a healthcare data platform. A strong opening question for the rep: *As Health Catalyst scales Ignite's API layer across more health system clients, how is the engineering team currently handling structured logging and request traceability across those production services?*", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "detail": "redelivery (attemptNumber=1) or repeat within the batch of an eventId already processed"}
