{"stream": "process", "run_id": "run-07a33efb0ef94361", "event": "routing_decision", "ts": 1787759938.6338704, "trigger_id": "trg-8e984d8a2c115a45961c136e", "decision": "route", "agent": "email", "endpoint": "https://uniformly-spongy-unpadded.ngrok-free.dev/email/campaign", "route_id": "R2-lead-context", "property_name": "lead_context", "property_value": "Innovaccer is committing $250 million to expand its AI agent platform, which automates workflows across prior authorization, revenue cycle management, and population health. The foundation of this is Gravity, a cloud-agnostic data and AI infrastructure layer that ingests and normalizes data from EHR systems, claims, pharmacy, labs, remote monitoring, and social determinants of health. Named a Leader in the inaugural 2026 Gartner Magic Quadrant for Healthcare Provider Industry Cloud Platforms, the company is operating at a moment when most health systems and payers are still struggling to move AI beyond pilots and into enterprise workflows. This makes API observability acutely relevant: as Innovaccer deploys more agentic workflows across clinical and financial systems, logging is critical for understanding and debugging production APIs, and for APIs specifically, it breaks down into request logs — recording individual requests and responses — and application logs, which capture detailed messages from within the code. Bridging the gap between aggregated API metrics and request-level debugging is essential; when anomalies surface in metrics, teams need to drill down to the relevant requests to understand exactly what happened. For a platform serving health systems like Orlando Health, Adventist HealthCare, and Banner Health, the stakes of an uninspected API failure in a care workflow are high. Opening question: As Innovaccer's engineering teams scale the number of AI agents running across clinical and revenue cycle workflows, how are they currently handling request-level observability and log correlation when something breaks in production?", "event_id": "435169886", "object_id": "534035230400", "attempt_number": 6, "basis": "property + value matched a route in agents_registry.yaml"}
